\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The half-turn commutant permits mixing within a sector}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{For the real [half-turn](fdip-000L), define its commutant by
#{\operatorname{Comm}(P)=\{R\in\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb R}(V):RP=PR\}}.
Restriction to the two sectors gives an algebra isomorphism
##{\operatorname{Comm}(P)
 \cong\operatorname{End}(V_+)\times\operatorname{End}(V_-)
 \cong M_2(\mathbb R)\times M_2(\mathbb R).}
If #{R} commutes with #{P} and #{Pv=\varepsilon v}, then
#{PRv=RPv=\varepsilon Rv}, so it preserves each sector.
Conversely, a map preserving both sectors obeys
#{PR(v_++v_-)=Rv_+-Rv_-=RP(v_++v_-)}.
The inverse to restriction acts separately on the unique sector
components; composition and the unit are componentwise. The
[explicit bases](fdip-000L) identify each endomorphism algebra
with #{M_2(\mathbb R)}.}

\p{In sector coordinates #{P=\operatorname{diag}(I_2,-I_2)}.
For #{R=\begin{pmatrix}A&B\\C&D\end{pmatrix}}, direct comparison gives
##{RP=PR\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad 2B=2C=0
 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad B=C=0.}
The group-algebra image is the strictly smaller algebra
##{\{\operatorname{diag}(xI_2,yI_2):x,y\in\mathbb R\}.}
Indeed #{aI+bP} has #{x=a+b}, #{y=a-b}, and every pair is
obtained from #{a=(x+y)/2}, #{b=(x-y)/2}. The map swapping
#{b_{+,0},b_{+,1}} and vanishing on #{V_-} belongs to the
commutant but is not scalar on #{V_+}, proving strict containment.}

\p{Each sign occurs twice, so an operator commuting with #{P} may
mix the two vectors in either sector. Symmetry therefore does not
force #{Q_\pm} to be scalar; the
[cycle precision blocks](fdip-003S) exhibit the freedom explicitly.
The group-algebra/module convention comes from
\citet{Sections 3.1-3.2, pp. 39-41}{sengupta2010representations};
the commutant calculation is direct. The
[interpretation boundary](fgap-0013) remains: this finite algebra
calculation selects no continuous gauge group, physical mass spectrum
or interaction.}
